What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/
Date:		October, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.5
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		One directory per instance of a CXL Hot-range Monitoring Unit
		(CHMU, CXL r4.0 section 8.2.8): block Y of memdev X, instance Z.
		An instance counts accesses to units of the device's memory and
		reports the units whose count crossed a threshold in a hotlist.
		It has one user at a time, whichever takes it first: see
		owner.

		Besides the perf PMU, in-kernel clients of the driver can use
		an instance (include/cxl/hmu.h). A client holds the instance
		from its claim to its release, runs it with the configuration
		staged in the files below for that time, and reads the hot
		units as host physical address ranges. Each client documents
		when it takes an instance and how it uses it.


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/owner
Date:		November, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.5
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RO) Who holds the instance: "perf" while a perf event is open
		on the instance's PMU (CONFIG_CXL_HMU_PERF), from
		perf_event_open() until the event is closed, whether or not
		it is enabled; the name of the in-kernel client that holds it;
		else "none". There is nothing to write: an instance is taken
		by using it, first come, first served, and whoever comes
		second does not get it: perf_event_open() fails with -EBUSY,
		and so does the claim of a client. An instance cannot be
		reserved for a user that is not using it.


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/unit_size
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/hotness_threshold
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/epoch_us
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/range_base
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/range_size
Date:		November, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.5
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RW) The configuration an in-kernel client claims the
		instance with. Only a write to these files changes them. A
		perf event is configured by its own attributes and neither
		reads nor changes them. A store takes effect at the next
		claim by an in-kernel client; one that holds the instance
		keeps what it claimed with. Each value is checked against the instance's
		capabilities at store time: a value the instance does not
		support is refused with -EOPNOTSUPP, an out-of-range one with
		-EINVAL or -ERANGE, and a refused store leaves the old value.
		Where a file has the name of a parameter of the instance's
		perf PMU (hotness_threshold, range_base, range_size) it has
		the same meaning and unit.

		unit_size: bytes per counted unit, a power of two from
		supported_unit_sizes. The default is 4096 if supported, else
		the smallest. (The PMU's hotness_granual is its log2.)

		hotness_threshold: the count at which a unit is reported,
		default 1. It must be below 1 << (the counter width), which
		the device only reports once the unit size is programmed, so
		a value that is too large is refused with -EINVAL by the
		claim, not by the store; with dynamic debug enabled for the
		driver the kernel log names the width.

		epoch_us: the epoch length in microseconds, clamped to
		epoch_range_us and rounded down to what the device encodes
		(a multiplier of 1 to 4095 on a scale of 100 us, 1 ms, 10 ms,
		100 ms or 1 s); reads back the effective value. The default
		is one second. 0 is -EINVAL; a length the device cannot
		encode is -ERANGE.

		range_base, range_size: the device physical address range
		counted, in units of 256 MiB; base + size must not exceed
		capacity_ranges, size must not be 0 and the range must hold at
		least one unit. The default is the whole device.

		What has no file is fixed for an in-kernel client: epoch mode
		if the instance supports it, else always-on; reads and writes
		of any kind if supported, else non-TEE reads and writes, else
		the first access type the instance offers; the smallest
		down-sampling factor, not randomized.


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/supported_modes
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/supported_unit_sizes
